Intending to start a business in Germany, you must know a few things to get started. There are various options for your business in Germany:
Limited liability company (GmbH) – It means that the business is a separate entity to the individuals who form it. The liabilities of the business are kept scattered to the founders’ personal affairs.
Partnership – This model can be a common partnership in which the partners are liable for all the responsibilities of the company or a limited liability model. It’s possible to have a partnership in which one partner is a general partner and another limit their liabilities to the amount they initially invest in the company.
In Germany, some forms of the corporate entity need a minimum investment from founders. A Limited liability company has a minimum initial stock amount of EUR25.000, while a corporation needs EUR50.000.
Legal obligations and responsibilities Some trades and professions in Germany are covered by permits and licenses; this means, that for working in this field, you must have set qualifications.
If your business is in one of above mentioned, check out, that before starting to register your business, which permits you must present. If you employ others in your business you have to calculate and pay deposits for things such as unemployment insurance for them.
