News - CCTV Finance: Prices of flat panel TVs have risen more than 10% this year because of the tight supply of raw material
CCTV Finance: Prices of flat panel TVs have risen more than 10% this year because of the tight supply of raw material
However, due to the rising prices of raw materials and the tight supply of display panels, the average price of TV sales has increased much during this year's May Day, compared with previous years.
Jin Liang, general manager of Beijing Gome, told reporters that panels account for 60 to 70 percent of the cost of the complete appliance, and changes in the price of panels will directly lead to application price increases, which have continued to rise in the recent period, with an average increase of 10 to 15 percent compared with the beginning of the year.
CCTV financial report said that polarized film is the core display material of flat panel TV panels. In the world's largest polarized film production enterprises, the first quarter of the year-on-year growth of more than 20%, is still in the state of full production and sales.
Industry analysis believes that, considering the polarized film, glass substrate, driving IC and other raw materials are still out of stock, but overlay panel demand of the slack season is not light.
LCD panel supply and demand will be tight throughout the whole 2021.
Increase of three major applications, namely TV, laptop and monitor, accelerated from March to late April, with the average price increase of TV panel exceeding 6 percent.
In the first half of this year, laptop supplies have also been affected by a chip shortage.
They also analyse that top brand suppliers such as HP, Lenovo, Dell, Acer and Asustek Computer have turned to sourcing chips in shortage directly by themselves, rather than through ODM. This helps to shorten the component procurement process while giving suppliers more flexibility and control over supply chain management.
Industrial chain personage has previously revealed that consumer demand for notebook computers is still strong this year, which pulls up the demand for chips, panels. Shipments of laptop panels are expected to grow 4.8 percent year on year this year, and suppliers have set higher shipment targets.
