To this situation must be added the fall of several streaming reading services, such as the famous Oyster or Skoobe. And in this situation, Amazon, which leads the sector with Kindle Unlimited, has decided to take another turn of the screw with a new service called Amazon Prime Reading.
The money bag for the payment of readings per page, something that made Amazon's first streaming e-book service famous, is not initially available on this service, that is, Amazon negotiates directly with the authors and they decide what price to set for the rental or loan of the ebooks and do not create a bag with all the income from the service and set the price per page reading.
At the moment this is the main difference between the two services. There are other differences such as the price of the service or the titles that can be read simultaneously.
An important function within Amazon services is the number of ebooks that we can read simultaneously. While Kindle Unlimited allows you to read several ebooks at the same time although not on all the devices that we would like, Amazon Prime Reading only allows you to read 10 titles at a time, having to leave a title if we want to add a new ebook.
And you, what option do you get from these flat rates?
For the most demanding users with their readings, Amazon Prime Reading is a great option, possibly many of these titles have already read them. But it is true that the price vulture flies around Amazon Prime Reading.
Personally, I lean towards the flat rate of Kindle Unlimited and would recommend this service both for users who are looking for curated readings and for users whose readings with low quality are indifferent Why?
But, if we really want to get good readings quickly or we are users who buy everything through the Amazon store, perhaps the best thing for the moment is Amazon Prime Reading, not only for its readings but also for free shipping that Amazon still offers for its more premium customers.
