In recent months, streaming reading services have been the protagonists of the publishing world, eReader world, library world ...In general, everything related to Reading. It stops us less, since it offers the same as a library but in a faster way and without having to queue or wait for a book.
Given all this and taking into account its fondness for offering services to its customers, Amazon decided to create a streaming reading service, while respecting the rules of the other "Flat rates for ebooks". Thus was born Kindle Unlimited, a service that until recently was only in the United States but is fleetingly being taken to other countries such as Spain.
In recent months, we can possibly say recent years, Amazon has not led the ebook market as it used to, this has not been an impediment for it to continue to bill billions in this market.
The launch we are talking about is Kindle Vella, a service that some will qualify as a cheap version of Kindle Unlimited but that I personally prefer to qualify as a child of this service.
It may seem a bit cumbersome, but it is like a television series taken to the world of reading where the first episodes are free and then you have to pay for it.
Kindle Vella is an Amazon project that they have been working on for several months, so we will not find few readings, but we have worked with thousands of writers from the ebook store to bring content to the new platform. We are curiously the opposite of what happens with Prime reading, which despite being quite old, still does not offer a catalog as extensive as Kindle Unlimited.
Something that has surprised many and that some do not believe. Obviously, Kindle Vella will come to Amazon tablets and their ereaders, even more so when the service receives the same name as the family of ereaders, but it is still striking.
We know that Amazon has spoken and worked with thousands of writers to create this service so I do not think that this service is something that disappears with time or that only remains with the United States market, so it will be a matter of time before reaches other Amazon devices and apps, but how will it get there? Amazon with Kindle Vella raises an option that may be interesting for writers, Amazon itself and more assiduous readers: prepaid reading.
24Symbols: ebooks for all tastes and all prices | All eReaders
There numerous online stores in which we can find eBooks, more expensive or less, simpler or less, but today we are going to talk about an interesting Spanish project: 24Symbols. If you know it from the beginning, you will have been able to observe the changes (for the better) that the project has undergone since the first beta that appeared almost two years ago (and it seems like yesterday).
It was born as a reading project in streaming and without DRM, at a time when it was "a bit" difficult to find a good catalog of quality e-books, and by quality I mean a correct layout and a careful edition, I do not go into the good or bad of the content. Amazon had not yet landed in Spain and the «One-click purchase» had not taken our finger.
Reading in streaming It was not particularly attractive to me, because I am aware of how much my eyes suffer from reading for a long time on a backlit screen, however the project was very attractive to me because of the ease of access and quality of content. Although with readers with Wi-Fi we can access our 24Symbols account and read taking care of our eyesight.
Keep in mind that when I say "download" it does not mean that the file is transferred "physically" to our device, but that we can read it without being connected to the web application, but not do anything else with the file: we cannot change the device without going through the web, we cannot rename it, modify it, change the format, nothing we can do with an electronic book that we do have physically on our device. reading.
Logical, right? Some of the points that are most interesting to me of 24Symbols: it allows us to filter the content to facilitate searches (you know, author, category, publisher or language), we can also create our own folders and organize them according to our preferences and gradually add the readings we want to do, the ones we have fact or the books we want to buy (basically it is the organization that I use).
