We analyzed the new Kobo Libra 2, a device that has Bluetooth and its audiobook store to meet the demands of users. We tell you what we think of this new Rakuten Kobo device and if it can really be worth it.
1 Design: Standardizing the Kobo Product Range
3 The audiobook arrives with some work ahead
The first thing that catches the attention of this Kobo Libra 2 is its resemblance to the "older brother", the Kobo Sage, saving the differences in size and functionality. For starters, this new Kobo Libra 2 has dimensions of144,6 x 161,6 x 9 mm, for me almost perfect measurements for daily use.
Interacting with the new audiobooks built into the Rakuten store is easy. We have several options when it comes to connecting our headphones Bluetooth Either play an audiobook that will invoke a configuration pop-up window for the headphones, or go to the new Bluetooth connection section located in the configuration section of the lower right corner of the Kobo Libra 2 within its user interface.
However, I have missed the integration of audiobooks with traditional versions, I mean by this that we could continue listening to a book from the same point we left it reading previously, and then resume the traditional reading where we had left its "audio" version. This would be especially decisive, at the moment Rakuten Kobo only shows two individualized versions depending on whether it is an audiobook or a "traditional" book.
It also integrates other already common Kobo technologies such as ComfortLight Pro It automatically adjusts the brightness of the screen as needed, as well as the warmth of the color to better fall asleep. For its part, TypeGenius offers us 12 different fonts with more than 50 different styles.
If you come and go continuously with your Kobo Libra 2, I see it essential to acquire one of these covers, not so when you go to make a home use, you could save the 39,99 euros it costs at the official Kobo store or at Fnac. Editor's opinion
