The idea of this tutorial is to disable javascript in the ereader so that how the ereader's web browser works go faster and smoother. But not everything is as beautiful or as easy as it sounds.
We must also say that for the moment, this tutorial only works with Kindle ereaders. And if it works on more models, it would be with eReaders, never with tablets.
Table of Contents 1 Why do you talk about removing javascript on my Kindle?
3 How to make the ereader browser work faster?
The browser that the Kindle has was born from an experimental project that was carried out in 2009, more than 12 years ago and since then it has not been updated or improved, so in addition to being an insecure tool for certain operations, does not work well with the most modern websites, which rely heavily on javascript.
I explain. While it is true that 40% of all Internet web pages are made with WordPress and removing Javascript would not affect to get information, the rest of the Internet pages do not use it and the trend within informational web pages is to use static sites that are mostly built and use javascript, so disabling the interpretation of this language would cause these sites to malfunction and not be able to receive this information.
3 Alternatives to this trick just as good Afortunadamente there are more alternatives to this trick that will allow us to read our web pages in a faster, safer way and without losing functionalities.
For Kindle users we have the option of SendtoKindle and for users of other devices we have the option of Pocket. Both are very good and not only allow us to read the web pages in our ereader as if they were an ebook but we can also use the dictionary, underline or change the color.
The third alternative is the oldest but just as functional. It would be to download the news or the feed from the website through Caliber and that this send it as an ebook to our ereader.
