Overseas developed wireless sensors that can monitor sweat and pulse through RFID, which can be attached to the skin
These two kinds of sensors do not need the connection of the external circuit, and transmit the signal to the processor through the change of the internal current and the contact of the metal ink. They can accurately monitor body changes without affecting people’s normal exercise, and analyze and process the data.
Stanford’s elastic wireless “BodyNet” sensor is made of a flexible material covered with metallic ink, which is similar in appearance to a band-aid.
If you put BodyNet on a joint like a sticker, it can report whether that joint is bent and how much it is bent. The sensor system is very sensitive and can also detect the subtle changes that the skin undergoes with each heartbeat, or the broader changes that accompany breathing.
Although there are similar devices that can be clipped to clothes, they are still not used to monitor physiological data in the gym. Stanford University professors said they will create a whole-body skin sensor array that can collect physiological data without interfering with people’s normal exercise.
Normally, sweat monitors collect sweat every fifteen minutes and then analyze them in batches. But such monitors cannot analyze the changes in sweat every minute or less.
The head of the skin sensor project and a professor at Berkeley University, Ali Javey, said that the goal of this project is not just to make sensors, but to start research on more topics to see what sweat can tell us. He said: “Decoding the sweat composition requires reliable and repeatable sensors.
Sensors that can be attached to the skin provide a more convenient way for research on human physiological data. Patch sensors can be placed on different body parts to provide more accurate data for different research projects.
