The first barcode patent was issued in 1952, and 22 years later, the first barcode was scanned in a retail store. Thirty-three years later, barcodes are still widely used, and no one thinks that RFID will replace barcodes.
Ultrasound: When you need room-level accuracy, this technology is very useful for real-time positioning of high-value assets. But like infrared tags, this technology requires batteries, so it is not suitable for low-priced items.
Two-dimensional barcodes and other forms of visual identification codes: These tags also require line of sight, which means that data cannot be read when the reader is not fully aligned with the tag. Therefore, these technologies are not suitable for reading the contents of the box.
At the end of the supply chain, tracking billions or even trillions of low-value products requires low-cost, line-of-sight, and no additional power supply technology. Passive RFID is the only technology that meets these requirements.
Will technology replace RFID in the future? Of course, this day will always come, but I doubt that I will see this day in my lifetime, because I am sure that RFID will not be out of date in the next 30 years or more.
