During the epidemic control period, Hangzhou Airport has introduced a batch of health ETC smart devices, which are used to check the health code of incoming passengers. During the security check, passengers only need to put their ID cards on the health ETC device for identification, and the screen will display the passenger’s latest health code information and broadcast a reminder.
The popularity of RFID has been greatly improved, and the application scenarios have been deepened simultaneously
Through the NFC function on the mobile phone, Chongqing Jiangbei International Airport has successfully integrated the electronic ID card and the boarding pass into one, allowing passengers to complete the security check and boarding through the “one code to the end”.
It is understood that the T3A baggage handling system of Jiangbei Airport is the first baggage handling system in China to use “open-loop (chip not recycled)” RFID radio frequency identification technology. This is mainly due to two reasons: on the one hand, the application cost of RFID tags is continuously reduced, and on the other hand, today’s RFID identification system has increased the identification accuracy rate to more than 99%, significantly improving work efficiency and reliability.
Since 2019, the Civil Aviation Administration of China has launched a special campaign of “Civil Aviation Service Quality Key Issues” and requested to promote the construction of a full-process baggage tracking system. Domestic airports and airlines have significantly accelerated the rhythm of the launch of RFID projects, but they did not expect to encounter the sudden impact of the epidemic.
Through these cases, we can not only see that the enthusiasm of the domestic aviation industry to introduce RFID technology has increased significantly, but also can see the continuous deepening of these projects in specific application links. For example, in the air luggage transportation, RFID technology has been used in various segments such as sorting, consignment, and tracking.
Hong Kong Airport Authority has considered deploying RFID technology as early as 2003, hoping to improve the throughput efficiency of luggage and parcels with the help of RFID technology. However, because the application cost at that time was too high, and the successful reading rate of the system was not ideal, it was not widely adopted by airlines.
